<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tlaloc</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tlaloc was the Aztec god of rain, storms and agricultural fertility, recognisable by his goggle eyes and fangs. A storm god with similar features was already worshipped at Teotihuacan in the first centuries CE, and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a> Tlaloc shared the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/templo-mayor">Templo Mayor</a> with the war god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huitzilopochtli">Huitzilopochtli</a>. Offerings, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/child-sacrifice">child sacrifices</a>, were made to him on mountains and at springs to secure rain.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tlaloc.jpg" alt="Tlaloc" width="320" height="403" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tlaloc_glyph.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
